Desktop items in a fixed grid, with rounded boxes, a blurred label shadow and a shortcut badge.

The grid is compact: 48-pixel icons in cells 76 pixels wide, names wrapped
to two lines of about 70 pixels, a little air above the icon and between it
and its name, and a few pixels between rows so that two selected items'
boxes stay apart.  Upstream's cells were 116 pixels wide, which spread the
icons out more than a desktop of shortcuts needs.

Every item's box is the width of its cell, whatever the length of its name:
the icon's rectangle now spans the cell from its top to the label, and the
label's spans the cell's width.  Those two rectangles are what is drawn,
repainted and hit-tested, so they stay one shape.  The boxes and the
keyboard focus frame have 8-pixel rounded corners.

The label's shadow is a soft blur -- the text laid down at low opacity in a
small cloud of offsets, densest one pixel down and right -- rather than two
hard copies.

A shortcut is marked with a small badge over the icon's bottom-right
corner: a disc in the LikeOS accent, ringed in white, with a white arrow
pointing up and to the right.  libfm's cell renderer would draw its own
black arrow in the top-left corner when given the file's info, so a
shortcut's icon is rendered without it.

